From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] init: do not make the cpuctl tasks files world-writable

init.samsung.rc set mode 0666 on three files of the cpu cgroup hierarchy:

  /dev/cpuctl/tasks
  /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/tasks
  /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/tasks

Writing a thread id to such a file moves the thread into that cgroup.
With the permission check this kernel had for the cpu hierarchy (it
allowed every writer, see kernel patch "cgroup: one credential rule for
moving another user's task"), any process that could open one of the
files could move any thread of any user there; SELinux still kept app
domains out (only "domain -appdomain" may write cgroup files), every
native daemon could.

Nothing needs the mode:
- bg_cached and bg_abnormal are groups of Samsung's own framework. No
  file of this tree other than init.samsung.rc names them, and neither
  does any blob under vendor/samsung (searched for "cpuctl", "bg_cached"
  and "bg_abnormal" in sources and binaries).
- Android's task profiles (system/core/libprocessgroup/profiles/
  task_profiles.json) never join the root group of the cpu controller;
  they use foreground, background, top-app, system, system-background,
  rt, dex2oat, camera-daemon, nnapi-hal and foreground_window, whose
  files init.rc sets to system:system 0664.
- The processes that move other users' threads are init, zygote and the
  power HAL (root), and system_server and lmkd (CAP_SYS_NICE, with the
  system uid or gid for the files).

Remove the three chmod lines. /dev/cpuctl/tasks keeps the 0664 that
init.rc has already given it when this action runs (init.rc's "on init"
is parsed first), the other two keep the kernel's default 0644; all
three stay owned by system through the chown lines above them, which
are unchanged.

Verified by reading only; not built, not run.
---
 rootdir/etc/init.samsung.rc | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/rootdir/etc/init.samsung.rc b/rootdir/etc/init.samsung.rc
index 0f6e767..004d4b5 100644
--- a/rootdir/etc/init.samsung.rc
+++ b/rootdir/etc/init.samsung.rc
@@ -22,7 +22,9 @@ on init
     mount cgroup none /dev/cpuctl cpu
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/tasks
-    chmod 0666 /dev/cpuctl/tasks
+    # (No "chmod 0666" on the tasks files of /dev/cpuctl, bg_cached and bg_abnormal any more:
+    # world-writable, they let every process move itself into these groups. Nothing needs it;
+    # /dev/cpuctl/tasks keeps the 0664 that init.rc gives it, the other two the kernel's 0644.)
     write /dev/cpuctl/cpu.shares 1024
     write /dev/cpuctl/cpu.rt_period_us 1000000
     write /dev/cpuctl/cpu.rt_runtime_us 950000
@@ -31,7 +33,6 @@ on init
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/tasks
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/cpu.cfs_period_us
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/cpu.cfs_quota_us
-    chmod 0666 /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/tasks
     chmod 0664 /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/cpu.cfs_period_us
     chmod 0664 /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/cpu.cfs_quota_us
     write /dev/cpuctl/bg_cached/cpu.rt_runtime_us 700000
@@ -43,7 +44,6 @@ on init
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/tasks
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/cpu.cfs_period_us
     chown system system /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/cpu.cfs_quota_us
-    chmod 0666 /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/tasks
     chmod 0664 /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/cpu.cfs_period_us
     chmod 0664 /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/cpu.cfs_quota_us
     write /dev/cpuctl/bg_abnormal/cpu.rt_runtime_us 700000
